# rules: swift-sql-built-from-untrusted-input
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-89.swift
# from: CWE-89 (SQL Injection), swift/sqllite/sqllite-injection-audit.yaml, MASVS-CODE-4
#
# An app's SQLite file is not a server's database and the impact is different
# rather than absent. The attacker is whoever can hand the app a URL - another
# app on the phone, a web page, a scanned code - and what an injected query
# gets them is every row the app can read, which is usually every account it
# has ever cached.
#
# `sqlite3_bind_text` against a `?` placeholder is the fix, and it is less code
# rather than more: a bound value is never quoted, never escaped and never
# parsed as SQL.
#
# The rule follows the value rather than matching a shape, because neither end
# is a finding on its own: reading a deep link is what an app with a URL scheme
# does, and running a query is what an app with a database does. What is wrong
# is that the one reached the other.
#
# That flow could not be followed at all before xen0bit/pwrq#56. Swift labels
# a `for` loop's two halves `item` and `collection` - a pair no other grammar
# here uses - so a value walked out of a collection carried nothing, and
# `for item in url.pathComponents` is how an app reads a deep link's path.
#
# The sources are the three ways a URL hands over an attacker's string, and
# they are read off the receiver as well as the property: `resources.getString`
# taught the Kotlin pass that a bare property name is every string in the app.
# Here `lastPathComponent`, `pathComponents`, `queryItems` and `absoluteString`
# are specific enough to stand on their own, and `host` and `path` are not,
# which is why they are absent.
#
# What the user typed is a source too, and it needs a guard the URL sources do
# not. `.text` is a property on half the types in UIKit, so the receiver has to
# look like an input control - this is the same guard the Kotlin pass had to
# put on `getString`, where a bare property name turned out to be every string
# in the app. OWASP's iGoat writes the textbook case,
# `"%" + "\(searchField.text!)" + "%"` into a `LIKE`, and it is what this
# addition is for.
#
# `\(limit)` where limit is an Int is not reported, and that is the taint
# engine's doing rather than a test here: the value never came from outside.

[ "$$$U.lastPathComponent", "$$$U.pathComponents", "$$$U.absoluteString",
  "$$$U.queryItems", "$$$U.dataString", "$$$U.fragment", "$$$U.query",
  "UIPasteboard.general.string", "$$$P.string(forKey: $K)" ] as $fromOutside
| ["$$$F.text"] as $typedIn
| "(?i)field|input|search|query|box|entry|term|name|email|user" as $isAnInputControl
| [ "sqlite3_exec($DB, $SQL, $$$_)", "sqlite3_prepare_v2($DB, $SQL, $$$_)",
    "sqlite3_prepare($DB, $SQL, $$$_)", "sqlite3_prepare_v3($DB, $SQL, $$$_)",
    "$$$C.execute($SQL)", "$$$C.run($SQL)" ] as $runs
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $fromOutside + $typedIn + $runs) as $all
| ( ($all | of($fromOutside))
    + ($all | of($typedIn) | where_capture("F"; $isAnInputControl)) ) as $untrusted
| ( $all | of($runs) | focus("SQL") | reaching($untrusted; []) )
| finding("swift-sql-built-from-untrusted-input";
    "this query is assembled from a value the caller of the app chose; bind it "
    + "to a ? placeholder with sqlite3_bind_text instead")
| report
